export default {
  intro: "Welcome to the Alapin Sicilian, a practical, low-theory weapon for White against 1...c5. With 2.c3 we prepare d4 and aim to recapture with the c-pawn, building a broad pawn centre on d4 and e4 that gives us space and easy development. Against every Black set-up you will learn the key plans, the typical traps, and the moments where our central space turns into a concrete attack.",
  lineNames: {
    "1": "2...Nc6 3.d4 cxd4 4.cxd4 e6 5.d5: 8.Qe2+",
    "2": "2...g6 Fianchetto: 5...d6 6.h3, 10.Rc1",
    "3": "2...g6 Fianchetto: 5...Nc6 6.Nc3, 8.d5",
    "4": "2...g6 Fianchetto: 5...d5 6.e5 Bg4 7.Bb5+",
    "5": "2...g6 3.d4 cxd4 4.cxd4 d5 5.e5: 11...Nxe5? 13.Bxh6",
    "6": "2...g6 3.d4 cxd4 4.cxd4 d5 5.e5: 11...e6 14.Na4",
    "7": "2...g6 3.d4 cxd4 4.cxd4 Bg7: 6...Nb8 7.d6!?",
    "8": "2...g6 3.d4 cxd4 4.cxd4 Bg7: 6...Ne5 7.f4",
    "9": "2...e5 3.Nf3 d6: 6...Nf6, 11.Nf5",
    "10": "2...e5 3.Nf3 d6: 6...Nc6, 12.Bf1",
    "11": "2...e5 3.Nf3 Nc6 4.Bc4 d6: 11.Qxd4!",
    "12": "2...e5 3.Nf3 Nc6 4.Bc4 Nf6: 6...Na5",
    "13": "2...e5 3.Nf3 Nc6 4.Bc4 Nf6: 6...Nxd5",
    "25": "2...e6 3.d4 d5 4.exd5 exd5 5.Bb5+ Nc6: 9...Be6",
    "26": "2...e6 3.d4 d5 5.Bb5+ Nc6: 9...O-O 11.Qxd5",
    "27": "2...e6 3.d4 d5 5.Bb5+ Bd7: IQP blockade",
    "28": "2...e6 French Advance: 12...Ncxd4? 14.g4!",
    "29": "2...e6 French Advance: 12...g6 13.Bg5",
    "30": "2...e6 French Advance: 9...Nfxd4 11.Qg4",
    "31": "2...e6 French Advance: 8...O-O Greek Gift",
    "32": "2...e6 French Advance: 7...Nxd4 8.Qg4",
    "33": "2...e6 3.d4 cxd4 4.cxd4 Nf6: Greek Gift",
    "44": "2...Nc6 3.d4 e6 4.d5: 7.Qe2+",
    "45": "2...Nc6 3.d4 e6 4.d5: 5...Ne5 6.Nf3",
    "46": "2...Nc6 4...g6 5.d5 Nb8 6.Qd4: Knight Trap",
    "47": "2...Nc6 4...g6 5.d5 Ne5 6.f4",
    "48": "2...Nc6 4...d6 5.d5 Ne5 6.f4: 9...Bxe6? 10.f5",
    "49": "2...Nc6 4...d6 5.d5 Ne5 6.f4: 9...fxe6",
    "50": "2...Nc6 4...d6 5.d5 Ne5 6.f4 Nd7: 12.g4",
    "51": "2...Nc6 4...e6 5.d5 exd5: 6...Ne5, 12.d6!",
    "52": "2...Nc6 4...e6 5.d5 Ne5 6.f4: 10.Nb5",
    "53": "2...Nc6 3.d4 cxd4 4.cxd4 d5 5.exd5",
    "54": "2...Nc6 4...Nf6 5.d5 Ne5 6.f4 Ng6 7.e5",
    "55": "2...Nc6 3.d4 d5 4.exd5",
    "56": "2...d6 5...g6: 9.d5 Ne5 10.Nxe5",
    "57": "2...d6 5...e6: Scheveningen Set-up, 9.Qe2"
  },
  notes: {
    "e4": "1.e4 grabs the centre and opens lines for the queen and the f1 bishop. Against it Black chooses the Sicilian, and our weapon is the Alapin with 2.c3.",
    "e4 c5": "1...c5 is the Sicilian: Black fights for d4 from the side and avoids symmetry. In the main Open Sicilian White plays 2.Nf3 and 3.d4, but we take a simpler, more strategic road.",
    "e4 c5 c3": "2.c3 is the Alapin. The point is simple: we prepare 3.d4, and if Black exchanges with ...cxd4 we recapture with the c-pawn, keeping two pawns side by side on d4 and e4. Black's usual Sicilian counterplay on the c-file and against a knight on d4 never arrives.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6": "2...Nc6 develops and adds pressure to d4 before we have played it. Black often follows with ...cxd4 and ...e6, ...g6 or ...d6, hoping our centre becomes a target rather than an asset.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4": "3.d4 carries out the main idea at once. The centre is ours to take, and Black has to decide how to react before we play Nf3 and develop calmly.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4": "3...cxd4 is the most natural reply: Black releases the tension and we get our two-pawn centre. Now the question is how Black chooses to attack d4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4": "4.cxd4 recaptures towards the centre. We have the classic Alapin dream: pawns on d4 and e4 and open lines for all our pieces.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6": "4...e6 prepares ...d5 or ...Bb4, but it leaves the knight on c6 a little exposed to a d-pawn push. That is exactly what we exploit next.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5": "5.d5! hits the knight on c6 and gains space. Black must decide whether to open the centre with ...exd5 or retreat the knight; the e-file opening is risky for Black because his king is still in the centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5": "5...exd5 opens the position. Black hopes to activate his pieces, but after we recapture his king is still on e8 and the e-file and our d-pawn will cause him problems.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5": "6.exd5 recaptures and attacks the knight on c6 again. The knight has no comfortable square, and Black has to watch out for tricks along the opened e-file.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Nce7": "6...Nce7 retreats the knight, but it blocks the f8 bishop and the e-file is now dangerous for Black. Be ready to push the d-pawn further.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Nce7 d6": "7.d6! hits the knight on e7 and drives a wedge into Black's camp. The pawn also controls e7 and c7, which sets up a powerful queen check next.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Nce7 d6 Nc6": "7...Nc6 is about the only sensible square, since the knight was attacked by the d6 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Nce7 d6 Nc6 Qe2+": "8.Qe2+! is the point. Any piece Black puts on e7 (queen, bishop or either knight) is simply taken by the d6 pawn, and 8...Ne5 runs into f4, winning the pinned knight. White wins material and Black's position collapses.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6": "2...g6 is the fianchetto set-up: Black wants ...Bg7 to aim at d4 and the long diagonal. It is solid, and our plan is to build the big centre and use our space.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4": "3.d4 occupies the centre straight away. Against the fianchetto our pawns on d4 and e4 blunt the coming bishop on g7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7": "3...Bg7 increases pressure on d4 before taking. Black hopes we have to make an awkward choice about our centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3": "4.Nf3 defends d4 and develops. Now if Black takes on d4 we recapture with the c-pawn and keep a full centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4": "4...cxd4 releases the tension. Black has to commit before we complete development.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4": "5.cxd4 gives us the ideal two-pawn centre. Black's bishop on g7 bites on granite as long as d4 stays solid.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d6": "5...d6 is a quiet, King's Indian style set-up. Black waits for ...Nf6, ...O-O and ...Nc6, while we use our extra space.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d6 h3": "6.h3 is a useful prophylactic move: it stops ...Bg4, which would pin our f3 knight and add pressure on d4. Our centre remains stable.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d6 h3 Nf6 Nc3": "7.Nc3 develops and protects e4 against the knight on f6. Our pieces go to their natural squares behind the big centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d6 h3 Nf6 Nc3 O-O Be3": "8.Be3 defends d4 a second time and prepares Qd2 or Rc1. Everything is solid and Black has no easy targets.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d6 h3 Nf6 Nc3 O-O Be3 Nc6": "8...Nc6 piles up on d4 with knight and bishop, which invites us to gain space with the d-pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d6 h3 Nf6 Nc3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5": "9.d5 kicks the knight and grabs space. The g7 bishop now hits a wall on e5 and c3, and Black's pieces have fewer good squares.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d6 h3 Nf6 Nc3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Nb8 Rc1": "10.Rc1 takes the open c-file. Black has been pushed back to b8, and we enjoy a pleasant space advantage; next we complete development with Be2 and O-O and keep Black's queenside breaks in check.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 Nc6": "5...Nc6 attacks d4 again and keeps the option of ...d6 or ...d5. We simply develop and keep the centre firm.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 Nc6 Nc3": "6.Nc3 develops and covers e4 and d5. We are not afraid of ...Bxd4 because the centre is well supported.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 Nc6 Nc3 d6 h3": "7.h3 again prevents ...Bg4, which would pin our knight and undermine d4. A small move that keeps everything under control.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 Nc6 Nc3 d6 h3 Nf6 d5": "8.d5 gains space with tempo on the c6 knight. Black's minor pieces are pushed back and the g7 bishop loses its targets.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 Nc6 Nc3 d6 h3 Nf6 d5 Nb8 Bd3": "9.Bd3 develops the bishop to its most natural square, supporting e4 and pointing at Black's kingside. Castling comes next.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 Nc6 Nc3 d6 h3 Nf6 d5 Nb8 Bd3 O-O O-O": "10.O-O tucks our king away. With development nearly complete, we can think about Be3, Qd2 and play on the queenside or in the centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 Nc6 Nc3 d6 h3 Nf6 d5 Nb8 Bd3 O-O O-O Nbd7 Be3": "11.Be3 develops the last minor piece and controls c5, a key square for a black knight coming to c5 to hit our d3 bishop. White has a comfortable space edge; ideas include Qd2, Rac1 and Nd4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5": "5...d5 hits e4 immediately. It is the most direct way to challenge the centre, but it lets us gain space with e5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5": "6.e5 gains space and shuts the g7 bishop out of the game. The structure resembles a French Advance where Black's dark-squared bishop is badly placed behind d4 and e5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4": "6...Bg4 develops and pins our knight, aiming to trade it off and weaken our hold on d4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+": "7.Bb5+ forces Black to block. We intend to trade on c6 and leave Black with a damaged pawn structure.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+": "8.Bxc6+ gives up the bishop to wreck Black's pawns. After ...bxc6 the c-pawns are weak and Black has a passive bishop on g7 blocked by our e5 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+ bxc6 O-O": "9.O-O safeguards the king. Black's light-squared bishop on g4 is his only good minor piece, and he has already lost time.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+ bxc6 O-O e6 Nbd2": "10.Nbd2 develops and prepares to reinforce f3 if Black takes there, keeping our pawn structure intact.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+ bxc6 O-O e6 Nbd2 Ne7 h3": "11.h3 asks the bishop a question. Black either retreats or gives up his good bishop.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+ bxc6 O-O e6 Nbd2 Ne7 h3 Bxf3": "11...Bxf3 trades the bishop. We recapture with the knight and keep our pawns intact.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+ bxc6 O-O e6 Nbd2 Ne7 h3 Bxf3 Nxf3": "12.Nxf3 recaptures, keeping the knight in play and maintaining control over d4 and e5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+ bxc6 O-O e6 Nbd2 Ne7 h3 Bxf3 Nxf3 O-O b3": "13.b3 prepares Ba3 and keeps the pawn chain solid. The bishop on a3 will be much stronger than Black's hemmed-in bishop on g7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 Nf3 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg4 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+ bxc6 O-O e6 Nbd2 Ne7 h3 Bxf3 Nxf3 O-O b3 Qb6 Ba3": "14.Ba3 hits the e7 knight, which has no defender, and controls the dark squares around c5 and f8. White has a comfortable edge: the better minor pieces, a strong e5 pawn, and long-term targets on c6 and the c5 square.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4": "3...cxd4 releases the tension at once, before we can play Nf3. We recapture with the pawn and keep the ideal centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4": "4.cxd4 gives us the full centre with pawns on d4 and e4. Black will try to hit it with ...d5 or ...Bg7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5": "4...d5 strikes at e4 immediately. We answer by gaining space with e5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5": "5.e5 gains space and closes the long diagonal for Black's future bishop on g7. Black will try to undermine our centre with ...f6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7": "5...Bg7 develops the bishop, though it now looks at our solid e5 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3": "6.Nc3 develops and puts pressure on d5. We keep the option of Nf3 to support d4 and e5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6": "6...Nc6 attacks d4 and prepares ...Nh6-f5 to pile up on it.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5": "7.Bb5 pins the c6 knight, reducing its pressure on d4 and e5. If Black ever takes on d4, the knight is pinned.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6": "7...Nh6 heads for f5 to hit d4. It keeps the f-pawn free to move for ...f6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3": "8.h3 keeps the knight out of g4 and prepares g4 to kick a knight from f5 later.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O": "8...O-O moves the king to safety, preparing ...f6 to break the centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3": "9.Nf3 develops and adds a second guard to both d4 and e5, so Black's coming ...f6 break does not win anything.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6": "9...f6 is Black's typical break, trying to open the f-file and attack our pawns.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O": "10.O-O completes our development before the centre opens. After ...fxe5 we will recapture calmly with the d-pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5": "10...fxe5 opens the f-file. We recapture with the d-pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5": "11.dxe5 recaptures and keeps a pawn on e5. Black now has to be careful about his loose knight on h6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5 Nxe5": "11...Nxe5? grabs a pawn but drops a piece: after the knights come off on e5, the g7 bishop no longer protects h6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5 Nxe5 Nxe5": "12.Nxe5 trades knights. When Black recaptures with the bishop, the h6 knight becomes undefended.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5 Nxe5 Nxe5 Bxe5 Bxh6": "13.Bxh6 picks up the knight, now also hitting the f8 rook. White is a piece up for a pawn and should win.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5 e6": "11...e6 is the sensible approach. Black stabilises d5 and plans ...Nf7 to attack our e5 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5 e6 Re1": "12.Re1 defends e5 a second time. Our centre pawn is the key to the position.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5 e6 Re1 Nf7": "12...Nf7 attacks e5 again with the knight, bishop and c6 knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5 e6 Re1 Nf7 Bxc6": "13.Bxc6 gives up the bishop to remove one attacker of e5, and leaves Black with weak, doubled c-pawns that cannot easily advance.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 Bb5 Nh6 h3 O-O Nf3 f6 O-O fxe5 dxe5 e6 Re1 Nf7 Bxc6 bxc6 Na4": "14.Na4 heads for c5, a dream square in front of Black's backward c6 pawn. White holds the e5 pawn and has a clear positional edge thanks to Black's weak c6 and e6 pawns and bad c8 bishop.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7": "4...Bg7 attacks d4 immediately. We defend it with a developing move.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3": "5.Nc3 develops and keeps control of d5. If Black attacks d4 more, we push the d-pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6": "5...Nc6 increases the pressure on d4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 d5": "6.d5 gains space with tempo. The knight on c6 must move and Black's pressure on d4 disappears.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 d5 Nb8": "6...Nb8 retreats the knight home, a very passive square. We can take advantage with an energetic pawn push.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 d5 Nb8 d6": "7.d6!? sacrifices a pawn to cramp Black. After ...exd6 Black has doubled d-pawns and the d6 pawn becomes a target.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 d5 Nb8 d6 exd6": "7...exd6 accepts the pawn, but now the d6 pawn is weak.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 d5 Nb8 d6 exd6 Nb5": "8.Nb5 threatens Nxd6+, a check that also hits the c8 bishop and the b7 and f7 pawns. White has excellent compensation: Black's pieces are undeveloped and the d6 pawn is hard to defend.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 d5 Ne5": "6...Ne5 centralises the knight instead of retreating, but on e5 it stands in front of our f-pawn and can be kicked with tempo.",
    "e4 c5 c3 g6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Bg7 Nc3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 f4": "7.f4 drives the knight away with tempo and gains even more space. Black's knight has no comfortable square, and our centre is very imposing.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5": "2...e5 stops our d4 plan directly. It leaves a hole on d5 and makes Black's d-pawn backward, which we can exploit.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3": "3.Nf3 develops and attacks e5 at once, keeping the d4 push in reserve for the right moment.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6": "3...d6 defends e5 solidly. We strike in the centre to open lines.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4": "4.d4 challenges the centre immediately. After the exchanges Black's d6 pawn will be isolated and the d5 square a permanent hole.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4": "5.cxd4 recaptures and keeps the tension. Black will usually take back on d4 with the e-pawn, opening the position.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4": "6.Nxd4 recaptures with the knight. Black has an isolated d6 pawn and we have a lead in development.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nf6": "6...Nf6 develops and attacks e4, preparing ...Be7 and castling.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3": "7.Nc3 develops and protects e4. The plan is Bb5+, castling and pressure against d6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Be7 Bb5+": "8.Bb5+ offers a trade of light-squared bishops. Black's light bishop is one of his key defenders of d5 and f5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Be7 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+": "9.Bxd7+ trades the light-squared bishops. Without that bishop Black has a harder time covering the weak d5 and f5 squares.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Be7 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Nbxd7 O-O": "10.O-O safeguards the king. Black still has the weak d6 pawn and holes on d5 and f5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Be7 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Nbxd7 O-O O-O Nf5": "11.Nf5 jumps into the hole created by ...e5 and ...d6. The knight attacks the d6 pawn and the e7 bishop and eyes g7, giving White a pleasant edge.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nc6": "6...Nc6 develops with a hit on our d4 knight, keeping ...Nf6 and ...Be7 in mind.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nc6 Nc3": "7.Nc3 develops and keeps control of d5. Our knight on d4 is well protected.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nc6 Nc3 Nf6 Bb5": "8.Bb5 pins the c6 knight against the king and prepares pressure on the isolated d6 pawn. The pin also stops ...Nxd4 ideas from coming with much effect.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nc6 Nc3 Nf6 Bb5 Bd7 O-O": "9.O-O completes king safety. With the king tucked away, our pieces are free to target the isolated d6 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nc6 Nc3 Nf6 Bb5 Bd7 O-O Be7 Bf4": "10.Bf4 puts the bishop on the diagonal towards d6, adding to the pressure on Black's isolated pawn and keeping Black tied up.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nc6 Nc3 Nf6 Bb5 Bd7 O-O Be7 Bf4 O-O Re1": "11.Re1 brings the rook to the centre, guards e4 and prepares e5 ideas against the d6 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 exd4 Nxd4 Nc6 Nc3 Nf6 Bb5 Bd7 O-O Be7 Bf4 O-O Re1 a6 Bf1": "12.Bf1 retreats the bishop, keeping it from being traded and maintaining control. White has a pleasant position: pressure against the isolated d6 pawn, a good square on d5 and solid development.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6": "3...Nc6 defends e5 and develops, keeping ...Nf6 and ...d6 or ...d5 in reserve. The drawback is that f7 and d5 can become sensitive once our bishop reaches c4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4": "4.Bc4 puts the bishop on its most active diagonal, aiming at f7 and the d5 square that ...e5 has weakened. Castling and Ng5 or d4 ideas follow.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6": "4...d6 is solid: it shores up e5 and opens the c8 bishop, but it makes ...d5 cost an extra tempo later.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O": "5.O-O gets the king safe before we open the centre. Our rook will soon be useful on the e-file.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6": "5...Nf6 develops and hits e4, but now Ng5 becomes possible with both minor pieces aimed at f7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5": "6.Ng5 lines up knight and bishop on f7. Black has to answer the threat, and the usual way is ...d5, which costs him the extra tempo he spent on ...d6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5": "6...d5 blocks the bishop's view of f7 and counterattacks, but it opens the centre while Black's king is still on e8.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5": "7.exd5 grabs the pawn. Black recaptures with the knight, and the centre starts to open while his king is still on e8.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5": "7...Nxd5 restores material, but the knight on d5 and the king on e8 now stand on open lines.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4": "8.d4! strikes at once. We want the e-file and d-file open while Black's king has not castled.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4 cxd4": "8...cxd4 accepts the challenge; our c-pawn recaptures and the c-file opens too.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4 cxd4 cxd4": "9.cxd4 keeps a pawn in the centre and invites Black to grab it on d4, which helps us open the e-file.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nxd4": "9...Nxd4 wins a pawn and removes our d-pawn, but now the e5 pawn and the e-file become the key issue.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nxd4 Re1": "10.Re1 lines up the rook against e5 and the black king behind it. The pawn on e5 is now pinned: if it ever leaves the file, the king is exposed to check.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nxd4 Re1 f6": "10...f6 hits the g5 knight and supports e5, but it does nothing about the pin on the e-file and weakens the light squares around Black's king.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nxd4 Re1 f6 Qxd4": "11.Qxd4! takes the knight because the e5 pawn is pinned by our rook and cannot recapture. If Black now takes our knight with ...fxg5, the d5 knight drops next.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nxd4 Re1 f6 Qxd4 fxg5": "11...fxg5 wins our knight back, but Black's pieces in the centre are loose and his king is wide open.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 d6 O-O Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nxd4 Re1 f6 Qxd4 fxg5 Qxd5": "12.Qxd5 wins the knight and White is a piece up for a pawn. Black's king is stuck in the centre, and Qf7 is a mating threat in many lines (for instance 12...Bd6? 13.Qf7#).",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6": "4...Nf6 develops and attacks e4, but it lets our knight jump to g5 with both pieces hitting f7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5": "5.Ng5 hits f7 twice. Black cannot defend it comfortably, so he usually counters with ...d5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5": "5...d5 blocks the bishop's diagonal and opens the centre, giving up a pawn in some lines for quick development.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5": "6.exd5 grabs the pawn. Now Black chooses between ...Na5, chasing our bishop, and ...Nxd5, recapturing at once.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5": "6...Na5 attacks our bishop and gives up a pawn for activity, like the Two Knights Defence. The knight on the rim is a long-term weakness, though.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+": "7.Bb5+ keeps the bishop on an active square with check. Black must block on d7 or c6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7": "7...Bd7 blocks, but now the bishop on d7 is pinned towards the king.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4": "8.Qa4 piles up on the pinned d7 bishop and also eyes the a5 knight. Black is forced to find defensive moves while we keep the extra pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4 b6": "8...b6 protects the a5 knight, but the pin on d7 remains.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4 b6 d3": "9.d3 calmly opens the c1 bishop and covers e4 and c4. The pin on the a4-e8 diagonal is still annoying, and Black has trouble untangling.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4 b6 d3 Nxd5": "9...Nxd5 regains the pawn, but our pieces are ready to exploit the d7 pin and the weak f7 square.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4 b6 d3 Nxd5 Bxd7+": "10.Bxd7+ cashes in on the pin, trading into an endgame where the f7 pawn will become vulnerable.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4 b6 d3 Nxd5 Bxd7+ Qxd7": "10...Qxd7 recaptures. The f7 pawn is now defended only by the king.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4 b6 d3 Nxd5 Bxd7+ Qxd7 Qxd7+": "11.Qxd7+ trades queens and drags the black king to d7, away from its defence of f7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4 b6 d3 Nxd5 Bxd7+ Qxd7 Qxd7+ Kxd7": "11...Kxd7 recaptures, leaving f7 undefended.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ Bd7 Qa4 b6 d3 Nxd5 Bxd7+ Qxd7 Qxd7+ Kxd7 Nxf7": "12.Nxf7 wins a pawn and hits the h8 rook, and the knight also eyes e5. White is a clean pawn up in an endgame where Black's knights are scattered and his pawns loose.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5": "6...Nxd5 recaptures at once and keeps material level, but the knight in the centre can become a target.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3": "7.d3 is a calm, useful move: it supports c4 and e4 and opens the c1 bishop. Now Qh5 or Qf3 ideas against f7 and d5 are in the air.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7": "7...Be7 develops and attacks our knight on g5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5": "8.Qh5 adds a third attacker to f7. Black has to weaken himself to defend.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6": "8...g6 kicks the queen, but it loosens the dark squares on the kingside.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3": "9.Qf3 attacks f7 and the d5 knight at the same time. Black has to give something back.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5": "9...Bxg5 removes our knight but leaves d5 hanging.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5": "10.Bxd5 takes the knight, keeping material level, and plants the bishop on a dominant central square.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5 O-O": "10...O-O safeguards the king and covers f7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5 O-O Bxg5": "11.Bxg5 takes back the bishop, so material is equal. Our bishop on d5 remains the best minor piece on the board.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5 O-O Bxg5 Qxg5": "11...Qxg5 recaptures. The queen is active but can be chased.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5 O-O Bxg5 Qxg5 Nd2": "12.Nd2 develops the last minor piece and heads for e4, where it will hit g5 and c5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5 O-O Bxg5 Qxg5 Nd2 Bg4": "12...Bg4 develops with tempo on our queen.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5 O-O Bxg5 Qxg5 Nd2 Bg4 Qg3": "13.Qg3 steps out of the attack and offers a queen trade on g5. Our pieces are well placed for the endgame.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5 O-O Bxg5 Qxg5 Nd2 Bg4 Qg3 Ne7": "13...Ne7 challenges our bishop on d5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 d3 Be7 Qh5 g6 Qf3 Bxg5 Bxd5 O-O Bxg5 Qxg5 Nd2 Bg4 Qg3 Ne7 Ne4": "14.Ne4 attacks the queen and the c5 pawn and heads for an ideal post. White is slightly better with the strong bishop on d5 and good central knight, while Black has weaknesses on c5 and the dark squares.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6": "2...e6 prepares ...d5 to strike at the centre. Black aims for a French-like structure where our c3 pawn is less useful.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4": "3.d4 occupies the centre. We are ready to meet ...d5 with an exchange or with e5, depending on what Black does.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5": "3...d5 hits e4 immediately in French style. We respond by exchanging, which opens the e-file and frees our pieces.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5": "4.exd5 exchanges in the centre. Black will recapture with the e-pawn and has an isolated pawn soon.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5": "4...exd5 gives Black an open e-file and a free c8 bishop.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+": "5.Bb5+ forces Black to block. We want to trade the bishop or win tempo before Black develops normally.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6": "5...Nc6 blocks with the knight, keeping the bishop pair, but the knight can be pinned and harassed.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3": "6.Nf3 develops and protects d4. We are happy to castle quickly and then decide whether to give Black an isolated pawn with dxc5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O": "7.O-O gets the king safe. The bishop on b5 keeps the c6 knight pinned and Black's development is slightly awkward.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5": "8.dxc5 removes the c-pawn and leaves Black with an isolated pawn on d5. The bishop recaptures, but the d5 pawn will need defending.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5": "9.Bg5 pins the f6 knight against the queen. That knight is the main defender of d5, so the isolated pawn comes under real pressure.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6": "9...Be6 defends d5 again and develops, but it gives our knight a target on e6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6 Nd4": "10.Nd4 hits the e6 bishop and adds pressure to c6, preparing a tactic on c6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6 Nd4 O-O Bxc6": "11.Bxc6! gives up the bishop pair to break Black's pawn chain: after ...bxc6 our knight can take on c6 with tempo on the queen.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6 Nd4 O-O Bxc6 bxc6 Nxc6": "12.Nxc6 grabs the pawn with tempo on the queen on d8. Black must find active counterplay to avoid simply being a pawn down.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6 Nd4 O-O Bxc6 bxc6 Nxc6 Qb6": "12...Qb6 counterattacks the c6 knight and the b2 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6 Nd4 O-O Bxc6 bxc6 Nxc6 Qb6 Bxf6": "13.Bxf6 removes the knight before it can defend, wrecking Black's kingside pawns with ...gxf6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6 Nd4 O-O Bxc6 bxc6 Nxc6 Qb6 Bxf6 gxf6 b4": "14.b4! is the key zwischenzug. It attacks the c5 bishop, which Black cannot keep together with winning our c6 knight; the trades leave material level.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6 Nd4 O-O Bxc6 bxc6 Nxc6 Qb6 Bxf6 gxf6 b4 Qxc6 bxc5": "15.bxc5 takes the bishop back. Material is equal again, but the pawn exchanges have left Black's structure in tatters.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 Be6 Nd4 O-O Bxc6 bxc6 Nxc6 Qb6 Bxf6 gxf6 b4 Qxc6 bxc5 Qxc5 Qf3": "16.Qf3 attacks f6 and d5. Material is equal, but Black's structure is wrecked: isolated pawns on a7, d5 and f6, and a weak king. White has a pleasant edge.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 O-O": "9...O-O castles but leaves d5 weak once the f6 knight disappears.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 O-O Bxf6": "10.Bxf6 removes the knight that guards d5. After the queen recaptures, the isolated pawn is simply loose.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Nc6 Nf3 Nf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Bxc5 Bg5 O-O Bxf6 Qxf6 Qxd5": "11.Qxd5 wins the d5 pawn. White is a clean pawn up; consolidate with Nc3 and development.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Bd7": "5...Bd7 blocks with the bishop and offers to trade the light-squared bishops, which frees Black's game a bit.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+": "6.Bxd7+ accepts the trade. Black will have an isolated d-pawn soon, and fewer minor pieces makes it harder for him to generate activity around it.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Nxd7 Nf3": "7.Nf3 develops and protects d4. Black's knight on d7 is a little passive.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Nxd7 Nf3 Ngf6 O-O": "8.O-O safeguards the king before we change the pawn structure with dxc5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Nxd7 Nf3 Ngf6 O-O Be7 dxc5": "9.dxc5 gives Black an isolated pawn on d5. The knight recaptures, but in the long run the pawn is a target and the d4 square is ours.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Nxd7 Nf3 Ngf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Nxc5 Be3": "10.Be3 develops with a hint of pressure on the c5 knight and takes control of d4, the blockading square in front of the isolated pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Nxd7 Nf3 Ngf6 O-O Be7 dxc5 Nxc5 Be3 O-O Nbd2": "11.Nbd2 develops the last piece and prepares Nb3 or Nd4 to blockade d4. White has a comfortable game: Black's isolated d5 pawn is a long-term weakness and with fewer pieces his activity fades.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4": "3...cxd4 exchanges first. After cxd4 Black often follows with ...d5, aiming for a French Advance structure, or ...Nf6 with pressure on e4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4": "4.cxd4 recaptures with the pawn, keeping a full centre with pawns on d4 and e4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5": "4...d5 attacks e4. We gain space with e5, reaching a French Advance type of position where Black's light-squared bishop on c8 is hemmed in.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5": "5.e5 gains space and keeps Black's pieces cramped. The f6 square is unavailable to Black's knight, and his kingside can become a target.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6": "5...Nc6 attacks d4, starting pressure on our centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3": "6.Nc3 develops and adds pressure on d5. Our plan is Bd3, Nf3 and castling, keeping the e5 wedge that cramps Black's kingside.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4": "6...Bb4 pins our knight and adds pressure on e4 and d4 indirectly.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3": "7.Bd3 develops towards Black's king. The bishop aims at h7, and it leaves d4 slightly underdefended, which sets traps.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7": "7...Nge7 develops, heading for f5 to attack d4. Watch for the Greek gift Bxh7+ if Black castles too soon.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3": "8.Nf3 develops and protects d4. Our knights and bishop now aim at the kingside, and Ng5 ideas become possible.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5": "8...Nf5 attacks d4 with both knights. It also blocks our bishop's view of h7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O": "9.O-O gets the king safe and deliberately leaves d4 under fire. If Black grabs it with a knight, Qg4 tricks come with tempo.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O": "9...O-O castles, but now the h7 pawn and the kingside are in our crosshairs.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3": "10.a3 questions the b4 bishop and gains queenside space. Black must either give up the bishop for our knight or retreat it to the a5-b6 diagonal.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2": "11.Bc2 keeps the bishop on the b1-h7 diagonal and prepares Qd3, a powerful battery against h7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3": "12.Qd3 sets up the queen and bishop battery against h7. The knight on f5 is now pinned against mate on h7, since moving it allows Qxh7#.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3 Ncxd4": "12...Ncxd4 grabs a pawn but runs into a nasty tactic, as the f5 knight is still pinned against h7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3 Ncxd4 Nxd4": "13.Nxd4 trades knights. Black recaptures with the bishop, but the f5 knight is now the target.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3 Ncxd4 Nxd4 Bxd4 g4": "14.g4! attacks the f5 knight, which cannot move without allowing Qxh7 mate. Black is going to lose the knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3 Ncxd4 Nxd4 Bxd4 g4 Bxc3": "14...Bxc3 takes a knight to reduce material, but the f5 knight is still doomed.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3 Ncxd4 Nxd4 Bxd4 g4 Bxc3 bxc3": "15.bxc3 recaptures. The f5 knight is pinned against h7 and cannot be saved, so White wins a piece.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3 g6": "12...g6 blocks the diagonal against h7, but it weakens the dark squares f6 and h6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3 g6 Bg5": "13.Bg5 occupies the weakened dark squares. The bishop eyes f6, and the queen can reach h6 later.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O O-O a3 Ba5 Bc2 Bb6 Qd3 g6 Bg5 Qc7 Ne2": "14.Ne2 adds a defender to d4 and prepares Nf4 or g4 ideas. White has a strong bind on the dark squares and kingside attacking chances.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O Nfxd4": "9...Nfxd4 grabs the pawn, but our queen gets active with tempo.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O Nfxd4 Nxd4": "10.Nxd4 trades a pair of knights. When Black's second knight lands on d4, our queen can hit it with tempo.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 Nf5 O-O Nfxd4 Nxd4 Nxd4 Qg4": "11.Qg4 hits g7 and the knight on d4 at the same time. Black cannot cover both comfortably, and ...Nf5 runs into Bxf5 followed by Qxg7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O": "8...O-O castles too early, allowing the classic Greek gift sacrifice.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+": "9.Bxh7+! is the Greek gift: with the knight ready to jump to g5 and Black's knight unable to reach f6, the sacrifice gives a winning attack.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+": "10.Ng5+ brings the knight in with check. Black's king must choose between g8, g6 and h6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6": "10...Kg6 is the most stubborn reply, keeping the king near the action.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4": "11.h4 threatens h5+ and brings the rook into the attack along the h-file.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+": "11...Bxc3+ gives back the bishop to slow us down.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3": "12.bxc3 recaptures with the pawn. Black's queen will try to counterattack c3 with ...Qa5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5": "12...Qa5 attacks c3 and threatens checks.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+": "13.Qd3+ defends the c3 pawn and checks the king at the same time. Black has to block with ...f5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5": "13...f5 blocks the check, but opens the e6 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5 h5+": "14.h5+ drives the king to h6, where a discovered check from the c1 bishop is waiting.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5 h5+ Kh6": "14...Kh6 is forced; the king walks into the discovered check.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5 h5+ Kh6 Nxe6+": "15.Nxe6+ is a discovered check from the c1 bishop, and the knight also hits the f8 rook.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5 h5+ Kh6 Nxe6+ Kh7": "15...Kh7 runs, but the knight can fork the rook.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nge7 Nf3 O-O Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5 h5+ Kh6 Nxe6+ Kh7 Nxf8+": "16.Nxf8+ wins the exchange with check. White is up a rook and two pawns for a bishop and has a winning position.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4": "7...Nxd4 grabs a pawn immediately. It's risky because our queen can attack g7 and the d4 knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4 Qg4": "8.Qg4! hits two things at once: the g7 pawn and the knight on d4 along the fourth rank. Black has to lose time dealing with both.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4 Qg4 Bxc3+": "8...Bxc3+ trades off our knight with check.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4 Qg4 Bxc3+ bxc3": "9.bxc3 recaptures, and the knight on d4 is still attacked by both our queen and the c3 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4 Qg4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Nf5": "9...Nf5 saves the knight and covers g7 at the same time, but the f5 knight can be removed.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4 Qg4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Nf5 Bxf5": "10.Bxf5 eliminates the knight that defends g7. After ...exf5 the g7 pawn falls and the h8 rook is exposed.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4 Qg4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Nf5 Bxf5 exf5 Qxg7": "11.Qxg7 wins back the pawn and hits the h8 rook. Black's king has lost its castling option and his dark squares are full of holes.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4 Qg4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Nf5 Bxf5 exf5 Qxg7 Qc7": "11...Qc7 counterattacks c3 and e5, hoping for ...Qxc3+.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 e5 Nc6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nxd4 Qg4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Nf5 Bxf5 exf5 Qxg7 Qc7 Ne2": "12.Ne2 protects c3 against ...Qxc3+ and develops, while the threat on the h8 rook remains. White is clearly better: Black's king is stranded in the centre and his kingside is in ruins.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6": "4...Nf6 attacks e4 and prepares ...Bb4 to put pressure on our centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3": "5.Nc3 defends e4 and develops. If Black pins with ...Bb4 we answer with Bd3, keeping the pawn defended.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3": "6.Bd3 defends e4 a second time and points the bishop at h7, which will matter if Black castles.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3": "7.Nf3 develops and protects d4. The knight is ready to jump to g5 in sacrificial lines.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5": "8.e5! kicks the f6 knight, the key defender of h7. This sets up the classic Greek gift.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5 Nd5 Bxh7+": "9.Bxh7+! is the Greek gift: with the f6 knight driven away, the bishop sacrifice opens Black's king to Ng5+ and our queen and h-pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5 Nd5 Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+": "10.Ng5+ follows up with check. The king has to pick a square, and each one has its own problem.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5 Nd5 Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4": "11.h4! brings the h-pawn and rook into the attack, threatening h5+ and pushing the king into more danger.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5 Nd5 Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3": "12.bxc3 recaptures with the pawn. Black's queen will try to counterattack the c3 pawn with ...Qa5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5 Nd5 Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+": "13.Qd3+ defends c3 with check and brings the queen into the attack. Black must block with ...f5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5 Nd5 Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5 h5+": "14.h5+ drives the king to h6, right onto the c1-h6 diagonal, where a discovered check is waiting.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5 Nd5 Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5 h5+ Kh6 Nxe6+": "15.Nxe6+ is a discovered check from the c1 bishop, and it also hits the f8 rook.",
    "e4 c5 c3 e6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Bb4 Bd3 Nc6 Nf3 O-O e5 Nd5 Bxh7+ Kxh7 Ng5+ Kg6 h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 Qa5 Qd3+ f5 h5+ Kh6 Nxe6+ Kh7 Nxf8+": "16.Nxf8+ wins the exchange with check, leaving White with rook and two pawns for a bishop and a continuing attack. White is winning.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6": "3...e6 keeps the tension and prepares ...d5, but it leaves the c6 knight exposed to a d-pawn advance.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5": "4.d5! gains space with tempo on the c6 knight. Black must react immediately, and his king is still in the centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5": "4...exd5 opens the e-file while Black's king is still in the centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5": "5.exd5 recaptures and attacks the knight again. The e-file is open, which favours us since Black has not castled.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Nce7": "5...Nce7 retreats the knight, but it blocks the f8 bishop and can be harassed by our pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Nce7 d6": "6.d6! hits the e7 knight and cramps Black's position. The pawn also controls e7, setting up the queen check.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Nce7 d6 Nc6": "6...Nc6 is almost forced, but it walks into the next blow.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Nce7 d6 Nc6 Qe2+": "7.Qe2+! wins material: any piece put on e7 is taken by the d6 pawn, and 7...Ne5 runs into f4, winning the pinned knight. Black is in serious trouble.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5": "5...Ne5 centralises the knight, hoping to annoy our pieces, but it can be exchanged.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nf3": "6.Nf3 challenges the strong knight. If Black trades, our queen recaptures with development and we keep the d5 wedge.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nf3 Nxf3+": "6...Nxf3+ trades knights, bringing our queen into play.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nf3 Nxf3+ Qxf3": "7.Qxf3 recaptures with a developing move. We have a space advantage with the d5 pawn, and Black's pieces are passive.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nf3 Nxf3+ Qxf3 d6 Bd3": "8.Bd3 develops and prepares castling. The bishop aims at Black's kingside and controls the e4 square.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nf3 Nxf3+ Qxf3 d6 Bd3 Nf6 O-O": "9.O-O safeguards the king and connects the rooks. We are ready to use the e-file.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nf3 Nxf3+ Qxf3 d6 Bd3 Nf6 O-O Be7 h3": "10.h3 is a useful prophylactic move, preventing ...Bg4 or ...Ng4 from harassing our queen and pieces.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nf3 Nxf3+ Qxf3 d6 Bd3 Nf6 O-O Be7 h3 O-O Re1": "11.Re1 takes the open e-file. White has a comfortable space edge with the d5 pawn and a lead in development; plans include Bf4, Nd2 and pressure on d6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6": "4...g6 aims for a fianchetto, hoping to pressure d4. We gain space instead.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5": "5.d5 kicks the knight and gains space. Black's bishop on g7 will hit a wall.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Nb8": "5...Nb8 retreats the knight, but now the h8 rook and the long diagonal are exposed.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Nb8 Qd4": "6.Qd4! attacks the h8 rook along the long diagonal (the g7 square is empty after ...g6). Black must block.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Nb8 Qd4 Nf6": "6...Nf6 blocks the diagonal, but the knight can be kicked.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Nb8 Qd4 Nf6 e5": "7.e5 kicks the f6 knight away. It has few good squares, and the h8 rook is exposed again once the knight leaves the diagonal.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Nb8 Qd4 Nf6 e5 Nh5": "7...Nh5 is the only way to keep the knight active, but it's on the rim.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Nb8 Qd4 Nf6 e5 Nh5 Nc3": "8.Nc3 develops and controls d5 and e4. We are now ready for g4, hunting the knight on h5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Nb8 Qd4 Nf6 e5 Nh5 Nc3 Bg7": "8...Bg7 develops and hits our e5 pawn with an x-ray towards the queen, but it takes away the knight's last retreat square.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Nb8 Qd4 Nf6 e5 Nh5 Nc3 Bg7 g4": "9.g4! traps the h5 knight: f6 is covered by the e5 pawn, f4 by the c1 bishop, g3 by our pawns, and g7 is occupied by its own bishop. White wins material.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Ne5": "5...Ne5 centralises the knight, but it stands in front of our f-pawn and can be kicked with tempo.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 g6 d5 Ne5 f4": "6.f4 drives the knight away with tempo and grabs more space. The knight has no comfortable square: ...Ng4 drops it to Qxg4, and ...Nd3+ or ...Nc4 is met by a bishop capture.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6": "4...d6 aims for a solid set-up. We push the d-pawn to gain space.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5": "5.d5 gains space and kicks the c6 knight. Black's pieces will struggle to find good squares behind our pawns.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5": "5...Ne5 centralises the knight, but it stands in front of our f-pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4": "6.f4 kicks the knight again with tempo. Our pawns on d5, e4 and f4 grab a huge amount of space.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6": "6...Ng6 puts the knight on the kingside, eyeing f4, but it is passive there.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3": "7.Nf3 develops and supports the centre, controlling e5 and preparing Nc3.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6": "7...Nf6 attacks e4 and prepares ...e6 to break up our centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3": "8.Nc3 develops and protects e4 against the f6 knight. Our pawn centre is very strong.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6": "8...e6 strikes at the d5 pawn, trying to undermine our centre before we complete development.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6": "9.dxe6 takes on e6, opening lines. How Black recaptures decides the course of the game.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 Bxe6": "9...Bxe6? recaptures with the bishop, but it walks into a pawn fork.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 Bxe6 f5": "10.f5! forks the e6 bishop and the g6 knight. Black loses a piece for at most a pawn or two.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6": "9...fxe6 recaptures with the pawn and keeps the pieces safe, but the e6 pawn becomes weak and Black's king is exposed on the e8-h5 diagonal.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+": "10.Bb5+ forces Black to block on d7, and the trade of light-squared bishops removes a key defender of e6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7": "10...Bd7 blocks, offering a trade of bishops.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4": "11.Nd4 attacks the weak e6 pawn and challenges the d7 bishop, which is its only defender.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5": "11...Bxb5 trades bishops, but now e6 is only defended by the queen.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5": "12.Ncxb5 recaptures and keeps both knights aimed at d6, c7 and e6. Black is under pressure.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5 Qd7": "12...Qd7 defends e6 and d6, but the queen becomes a target.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5 Qd7 Nxe6": "13.Nxe6! takes the pawn because 13...Qxe6 runs into 14.Nc7+ forking king and queen. The knight on e6 is a monster.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5 Qd7 Nxe6 Rc8": "13...Rc8 covers c7 to stop the knight fork.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5 Qd7 Nxe6 Rc8 Nbd4": "14.Nbd4 brings the other knight back to support the e6 knight and keep Black tied up.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5 Qd7 Nxe6 Rc8 Nbd4 Nxe4": "14...Nxe4 grabs a pawn back, but it opens the f-file further.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5 Qd7 Nxe6 Rc8 Nbd4 Nxe4 f5": "15.f5! supports the e6 knight and attacks the g6 knight. Black has to retreat again.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5 Qd7 Nxe6 Rc8 Nbd4 Nxe4 f5 Ne5": "15...Ne5 centralises the knight, but our pieces are ready to attack.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 dxe6 fxe6 Bb5+ Bd7 Nd4 Bxb5 Ncxb5 Qd7 Nxe6 Rc8 Nbd4 Nxe4 f5 Ne5 O-O": "16.O-O tucks our king away and brings the rook to the open f-file. Black's king is stuck in the centre with no f-pawn, and the knight on e6 dominates; White has a strong initiative.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7": "6...Nd7 retreats the knight, hoping to blockade on e5 later.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3": "7.Nc3 develops and supports e4 and d5. We are ready to hit the long diagonal with Qd4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6": "7...g6 prepares a fianchetto, but it opens the long diagonal towards the h8 rook.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4": "8.Qd4! attacks the h8 rook along the long diagonal. Black has to block with a knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4 Ngf6": "8...Ngf6 blocks the diagonal, but the knight can be kicked.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4 Ngf6 e5": "9.e5 kicks the f6 knight and reopens the long diagonal, hitting the h8 rook again.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4 Ngf6 e5 dxe5": "9...dxe5 takes the pawn, but now our f-pawn recaptures.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4 Ngf6 e5 dxe5 fxe5": "10.fxe5 recaptures, keeping a pawn on e5 that controls f6 and d6.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4 Ngf6 e5 dxe5 fxe5 Nh5": "10...Nh5 puts the knight on the rim, where it has few retreat squares.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4 Ngf6 e5 dxe5 fxe5 Nh5 Nf3": "11.Nf3 develops and supports the e5 pawn, preparing g4 to attack the h5 knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4 Ngf6 e5 dxe5 fxe5 Nh5 Nf3 Bg7": "11...Bg7 blocks the diagonal, but it takes away the knight's last retreat square.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d6 d5 Ne5 f4 Nd7 Nc3 g6 Qd4 Ngf6 e5 dxe5 fxe5 Nh5 Nf3 Bg7 g4": "12.g4! hits the h5 knight, which is running out of squares: f6 is covered by the e5 pawn, f4 by the c1 bishop, g3 by the h2 pawn. Black will lose material.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5": "6...Ne5 centralises the knight after the e-file opens, but it can be exchanged.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3": "7.Nc3 develops and supports d5. Our pawn on d5 cramps Black's position.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6": "7...d6 supports the e5 knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3": "8.Nf3 develops and challenges the e5 knight. We want to trade it off and use our d-pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6": "8...Nf6 develops and attacks d5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+": "9.Bb5+ checks and forces Black to block on d7, where the bishop will stay pinned against the king.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7": "9...Bd7 blocks, but the bishop is pinned against the king.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7 O-O": "10.O-O safeguards the king and brings the rook towards the e-file.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7 O-O Be7": "10...Be7 develops and prepares castling.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7 O-O Be7 Nxe5": "11.Nxe5 trades off Black's best piece. After ...dxe5 our d5 pawn becomes a passed pawn, ready to advance with tempo.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7 O-O Be7 Nxe5 dxe5": "11...dxe5 recaptures, but our d-pawn is now passed.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7 O-O Be7 Nxe5 dxe5 d6": "12.d6! attacks the e7 bishop. Capturing on d6 doesn't work, since the d-file opens for our queen, so the bishop must retreat.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7 O-O Be7 Nxe5 dxe5 d6 Bf8": "12...Bf8 retreats, but it undevelops the bishop.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 exd5 exd5 Ne5 Nc3 d6 Nf3 Nf6 Bb5+ Bd7 O-O Be7 Nxe5 dxe5 d6 Bf8 Re1": "13.Re1 pressures the e5 pawn and the e-file. Black's king is stuck in the centre and the d7 bishop is pinned, so White has a strong initiative.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5": "5...Ne5 centralises the knight before we can push e5, and refuses to open the e-file.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4": "6.f4 kicks the knight with tempo and grabs more space. The knight must retreat.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6": "6...Ng6 retreats the knight, eyeing f4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 d6": "7.d6!? cramps Black's position. The f8 bishop has no squares, and Black's development is hindered.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 d6 Qb6": "7...Qb6 attacks the g1 knight and stops Nf3.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 d6 Qb6 e5": "8.e5 gains space and supports the d6 pawn. Black's position is very cramped.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 d6 Qb6 e5 f6": "8...f6 tries to break our centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 d6 Qb6 e5 f6 Nc3": "9.Nc3 develops with a purpose: the knight is heading for b5, where it combines with the d6 pawn against c7.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 d6 Qb6 e5 f6 Nc3 fxe5": "9...fxe5 grabs a pawn, but our knight is ready to jump.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 e6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 d6 Qb6 e5 f6 Nc3 fxe5 Nb5": "10.Nb5! threatens Nc7+, and if the queen takes, dxc7 wins it for a knight. Black's position is very dangerous.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5": "4...d5 hits the centre at once, aiming for free piece play.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 d5 exd5": "5.exd5 takes the pawn. Black usually recaptures with the queen, and we gain time with Nf3 and Nc3, getting an open, active position.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6": "4...Nf6 attacks e4 and invites us to advance with gain of space.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5": "5.d5 kicks the c6 knight and gains space. Grabbing on e4 is risky for Black, since his c6 knight is also hanging.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5": "5...Ne5 centralises the knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4": "6.f4 kicks the knight again with tempo, grabbing space and preparing e5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6": "6...Ng6 retreats the knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5": "7.e5 kicks the other knight with tempo. Our pawns dominate the centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4": "7...Ne4 puts the knight on an outpost, but it can be harassed.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4": "8.Qd4 attacks the e4 knight, which has few retreat squares: d6 and f6 are covered by e5 and g5 by f4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4 Qa5+": "8...Qa5+ checks, trying to gain counterplay.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4 Qa5+ b4": "9.b4 blocks the check and gains space with tempo on the queen.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4 Qa5+ b4 Qc7": "9...Qc7 retreats the queen.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4 Qa5+ b4 Qc7 Be3": "10.Be3 develops, protects the queen on d4 and controls c5, a key square for Black's pieces.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4 Qa5+ b4 Qc7 Be3 f5": "10...f5 supports the e4 knight.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4 Qa5+ b4 Qc7 Be3 f5 Nd2": "11.Nd2 challenges the e4 knight, the only active black piece, and offers to trade it.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4 Qa5+ b4 Qc7 Be3 f5 Nd2 Nxd2": "11...Nxd2 trades knights.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 d5 Ne5 f4 Ng6 e5 Ne4 Qd4 Qa5+ b4 Qc7 Be3 f5 Nd2 Nxd2 Qxd2": "12.Qxd2 recaptures. White has a big space advantage and Black's pieces are passive; plans include Nf3, Bd3 and O-O.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 d5": "3...d5 strikes at the centre at once, aiming for open piece play.",
    "e4 c5 c3 Nc6 d4 d5 exd5": "4.exd5 takes the pawn. Black usually recaptures with the queen, and we develop with Nf3 and Nc3 with gain of time.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6": "2...d6 keeps things flexible and prepares ...Nf6 to hit e4. Black often aims for a King's Indian or Scheveningen type of set-up.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4": "3.d4 takes the centre straight away. If Black exchanges, we recapture with the c-pawn and keep two pawns side by side.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4": "3...cxd4 releases the tension; our c-pawn recaptures.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4": "4.cxd4 gives us the classic two-pawn centre on d4 and e4. Black's ...Nf6 will hit e4, so we must be ready to defend it.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6": "4...Nf6 attacks e4, forcing us to defend it.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3": "5.Nc3 defends e4 and develops. We have the big centre, and Black now picks his structure.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6": "5...g6 goes for a King's Indian set-up with ...Bg7. Black hopes to put pressure on d4 from the long diagonal.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3": "6.h3 stops ...Bg4 and ...Ng4, keeping our centre and pieces stable. The f3 knight will not be pinned.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7": "6...Bg7 develops the bishop on the long diagonal.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3": "7.Nf3 develops and defends d4. Our set-up is simple: knights on c3 and f3, bishops on e3 and e2 or c4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O": "7...O-O safeguards the king.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3": "8.Be3 defends d4 a second time and prepares Qd2. Our centre is rock-solid.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6": "8...Nc6 piles up on d4 with knight and bishop.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5": "9.d5 gains space and kicks the knight. Black's g7 bishop now faces a closed centre.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5": "9...Ne5 centralises the knight, hoping to annoy our pieces.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5": "10.Nxe5 trades knights. After ...dxe5 our d5 pawn is protected by e4 and cramps Black, while the g7 bishop is blocked by its own e5 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5": "10...dxe5 recaptures, but the g7 bishop is now blocked by the e5 pawn.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4": "11.Bc4 develops and supports the d5 pawn. The bishop is well placed behind our pawn chain.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4 a6": "11...a6 prepares ...b5 to kick our bishop.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4 a6 a4": "12.a4 stops ...b5 and gains queenside space, keeping our bishop on c4 safe.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4 a6 a4 Bd7": "12...Bd7 develops the bishop.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4 a6 a4 Bd7 O-O": "13.O-O safeguards the king. With development complete, we can focus on the queenside.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4 a6 a4 Bd7 O-O Rc8": "13...Rc8 takes the c-file and pressures c4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4 a6 a4 Bd7 O-O Rc8 Qb3": "14.Qb3 defends the c4 bishop and hits b7. It also supports a possible a5 advance.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4 a6 a4 Bd7 O-O Rc8 Qb3 Qc7": "14...Qc7 defends b7 and adds pressure on the c-file.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 h3 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Be3 Nc6 d5 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 Bc4 a6 a4 Bd7 O-O Rc8 Qb3 Qc7 Be2": "15.Be2 steps out of the pressure on the c-file. White has more space, a strong protected pawn on d5 and Black's g7 bishop is blocked by e5; plans include a5, Rac1 and Na4 or Nb5.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6": "5...e6 chooses a Scheveningen-style set-up, solid but passive. Black hopes to break with ...d5 or ...e5 later.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Nf3": "6.Nf3 develops and defends d4. Our big centre gives us more space.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Nf3 Be7": "6...Be7 develops and prepares castling.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Nf3 Be7 Bd3": "7.Bd3 develops and supports e4. We want to castle and then play Qe2 and Rd1.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Nf3 Be7 Bd3 O-O": "7...O-O safeguards the king.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Nf3 Be7 Bd3 O-O O-O": "8.O-O safeguards the king. Our pieces are all developed, and we can think about central pushes.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Nf3 Be7 Bd3 O-O O-O Nc6": "8...Nc6 develops and pressures d4.",
    "e4 c5 c3 d6 d4 cxd4 cxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Nf3 Be7 Bd3 O-O O-O Nc6 Qe2": "9.Qe2 supports e4 and prepares Rd1. White has a big centre and more space; plans include Rd1, e5 or d5 breaks to open lines against Black's king."
  }
};
